WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:14.230
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Welcome everybody to this
first meeting of the Joint Boards of NHS England

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.780
and NHS Improvement.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.530
We’re meeting here in public in London.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:26.279
The major audience for this will be on our
livestream, and it’s a great pleasure to

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:31.970
be able to welcome members of both boards
and to be accompanied today by my co chair,

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.149
Dido Harding of NHS improvement.

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:37.760
We will share the chairing of the session
today.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.579
We have only one hour, but it is a very important
hour.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:47.109
And I would like to preface the formal meeting
by saying that over the past few months we

00:00:47.109 --> 00:00:52.120
have been working closer and closer together,
between our chairs, between our chief executives,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.229
and between senior members of the team, and
now increasingly across the two boards.

00:00:58.229 --> 00:01:02.420
And we’re doing that for a number of reasons
which will become apparent during the course

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.269
of the discussions this afternoon.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:12.650
I think secondly, it’s important to emphasise
that this is the first step in what we see

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:17.140
as a programme of working more and more closely
together.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:22.930
We cannot, by virtue of the statutory framework
in which we work, merge the two organisations,

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:27.640
but there is a commitment across the board
that we should work as closely as possible

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:36.030
as we can under that legislation, in order
to overcome any of the obstructions that otherwise

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:42.890
exist to providing a seamless model of national
leadership across our two organisations.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:50.140
So, I’m going to kick off first of all by
actually declaring apologies for absence,

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.640
which we have from Dame Glynis Breakwell.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.420
I think everybody else is here.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.730
So let’s move straight away into the formal
business.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:04.070
I should say that we would record declarations
of interest normally, but we both have our

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.320
registers of interests.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.950
So may we take those as read?

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.430
Because the work that we’re looking at today
is at such a high level that I can’t conceive

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.879
that there would be a direct conflict, but
please do declare that should anything arise

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:18.700
in the course of discussions.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:25.850
So, three items on the agenda today are items
that we discussed yesterday in a very informal

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:30.880
development session between the two boards
in order to work them up into a state where

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.930
we thought that was proper that we could bring
them into a public session and have a formal

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.550
discussion around the table.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:42.270
So nobody should believe that these are papers
that come to us cold and raw; these are the

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:47.490
product of an enormous amount of work that’s
been going on between our two teams.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:53.740
With that by way of introduction, may I ask
you, Ian, to kick off, in the discussion of

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.180
the first paper which is the next steps on
NHSE/I joint working.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.760
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Thank you very much, Malcolm.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.780
And it’s a pleasure to be able to introduce
this item.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.120
I think this is a very significant moment
for us.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.230
The context for this discussion is clear.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:20.190
The NHS continues to deliver high-quality
care for its patients, but we know that out

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:28.700
there in the NHS, the response to an aging
population, a growing population, is about

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:34.580
working to join up care to create coherence,
and to create a focus for health in populations.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:41.959
That’s a development from a focus on individual
organisations, and I think the interesting

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:49.120
thing that we’re talking about here is that
as we as NHSI and as NHSE think about how

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:57.480
we support those front-line clinical developments,
we need to recognise that, good though the

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.910
work that both our authorities have done,
we also need to change.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:08.250
We need to align our work in a way that hitherto
we haven’t needed to do.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:14.930
I think if I recall back to the March board,
we signalled an ambitious intent to create

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:23.569
a joined-up leadership for the NHS in England,
with NHSE and NHSI aligning and working together.

00:04:23.569 --> 00:04:31.340
The proposals in front of us today outline
the actions that we said we would be working

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:39.740
on and then we would outline to effectively
move from what might be described as fragmentation

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:45.620
of leadership across the English National
Health Service to coherence; moving beyond

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:50.639
conflicting messages where that sometimes
happens between our organisations, to create

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:56.600
a single view of how the NHS is performing,
how it’s developing, how it’s spending

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:05.600
its money, and to mobilise our collective
resources for the next period of the NHS’s

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.740
history.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:16.400
For NHSI, it sits alongside a fairly radical
set of changes which are very much supportive

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:25.570
of our work with NHSE, but which actually
take NHSI, look at NHSI, and move us from

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:34.560
a predominant if not exclusive focus on regulation,
to gearing ourselves to be an agent of improvement

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.560
across the NHS.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:41.320
And so two sets of big changes: from a fragmented
national leadership to a coherent national

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:49.220
leadership; and for NHSI, a focus from ultimately
being a regulator, a commentator, a judge

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:54.310
and then an intervener, into an improvement
agency, an agency that is there on the pitch

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:58.380
in the front line supporting organisations
to improve.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.389
This needs to be done of course within the
current legislative framework, as Malcolm

00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:05.490
has said, but within that there’s no question.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:10.580
Our intent is clear: we want to join up leadership
for the NHS.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:17.650
What that means in a nutshell is as follows:
the creation of seven integrated regions for

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:25.120
both NHS Improvement and NHS England, led
by seven regional directors with integrated

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.810
teams.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:30.490
Regional directors reporting directly to Simon
and myself.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:38.139
And with a range of functions that is representative
of a very significant devolution of responsibility

00:06:38.139 --> 00:06:43.199
from London to closer to the front line of
the NHS.

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:48.820
I think that will be a significant and welcome
development for leading the NHS through its

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.580
next phase.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.100
Not to be outdone by what we’re doing at
the – proposing we do at the regional level,

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.930
we need to look at ourselves nationally as
well.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:04.640
And you will see here proposals for joining
up some of the key areas – medical, nursing,

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:09.600
finance – at the highest level to create
that coherent leadership that I think the

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.020
NHS will benefit from.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:18.710
I think in addition to that, we have worked
hard over the last few weeks to take what

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:23.150
might be described as a ‘one NHS’ approach.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:30.350
And that means that where functions are hosted
in NHS England or NHS Improvement, that increasingly,

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:35.370
that is hosted there but delivered on behalf
of the whole of the NHS, on behalf of both

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.710
NHS England and NHS Improvement.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:46.470
So, to give one example of that, a focus on
improvement for the NHS sits in NHS Improvement

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:51.510
in future, but that’s a whole NHS approach
to improvement.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:08:00.039
Sits alongside a series of significant changes
in the structure of NHS Improvement, creating

00:08:00.039 --> 00:08:05.639
a whole series of new posts to give us a more
improvement-orientated focus.

00:08:05.639 --> 00:08:12.410
A new NHS Chief People Officer, a Chief Provider
Strategy Officer, a Chief Improvement Officer,

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:18.400
and a Chief Commercial Officer; those being
many of the levers that, alongside the regional

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:24.930
directors and the joint directors of nursing,
medicine and finance, will be many of the

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:29.780
agents of change that we need to support coherence
in the NHS.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:39.990
Taken together, the corporate and the regional
directors will form part of a new joint executive

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:46.700
group – an NHS Executive Group; not a provider
or a commissioner, but an NHS Executive Group

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:52.959
– chaired by Simon and myself, in order
to provide a single leadership forum where

00:08:52.959 --> 00:08:58.610
we can take a full view of the NHS and its
development needs.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.820
Other than that, I would only say one thing
before finishing: that this is a significant

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:04.770
programme of change.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:05.800
It is important.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:11.080
It is the subject of a significant amount
of work, albeit there’s more to come.

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:16.020
It is about structures, it is about getting
structures right; however, it’s also about

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.830
ways of working.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:23.940
And our aim here is to create a coherent approach
to leadership across the NHS, to create the

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.020
kind of working cultures between our organisations
that will support the NHS best over the next

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:35.920
few years, and to do that with the right structures,
so that we can get alongside the need for

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:38.290
change at the local level.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:41.990
I think, Chairman, in the interests of time,
that’s probably enough from me.

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:45.260
Perhaps hand over to my colleague Simon at
this point.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.260
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Simon?

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.850
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, Ian and I are obviously
joined at the hip and the head on this, so

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:55.010
I’m not going to repeat all of the great
things that Ian has just said, and that are

00:09:55.010 --> 00:10:00.240
laid out here in the Board paper which has
been the product of a lot of joint work on

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.300
behalf of people all around this table and
our broader teams.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:08.530
I particularly want to thank Emily and Ben
and Hannah for the leadership that they’ve

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:11.360
shown, and maybe Emily wants to come in in
a moment.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:15.630
But also frankly, to thank our staff in advance
for what is bound to be an unsettling period

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.440
over the next four or five months.

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:22.940
We’re obviously going to have a chance to
have a conversation with all of the staff

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:27.500
in NHS Improvement and NHS England tomorrow
to deal with questions.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.790
Those of you who are watching on livestream
this afternoon, you’ll obviously have a

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:34.970
chance to digest the materials here overnight,
and then we’ll be able to talk more about

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:35.970
it tomorrow.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:40.060
But the reason we’re doing this, as Ian
said, is because frankly, we need to be the

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:41.630
change we want to see.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:46.920
And the change we want to see across the front
line of care is a set of services and organisations

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:52.320
which are much more joined up, where the fragmentation
and the pushing and pulling is replaced by

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.770
an approach to the health of populations that
just everybody sees is going to be required

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:02.870
to future-proof the health and care system
over the course of the next decade, which

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:06.959
is the kind of planning horizon we’re now
embarking on.

00:11:06.959 --> 00:11:12.490
So, practising what we preach, getting on
with the job, ensuring we’ve got the right

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:18.200
capabilities to do the big change programme
that the NHS is going to need over the coming

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.350
decade: that’s what this is all about trying
to position ourselves for.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.200
So, I think the detail is here.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:30.680
We will obviously be engaging with and consulting
our staff where it has direct impacts, and

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:33.000
Emily will be with colleagues leading that.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.290
So, I don’t know Emily, if you want to pick
up?

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.740
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Emily, by all means.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:38.970
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Sure.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:42.800
So, I think Ian and Simon have already covered
most of the content issues.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.130
I guess I was going to put a wrapper around
it in terms of first of all it is, as we’ve

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:51.480
discussed, the result of a huge amount of
joint work already, and there are lots of

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.080
people around this table who’ve put a huge
amount of time into it, but also a lot of

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.360
people who aren’t, who’ve worked very
long hours to do it.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:01.089
And what that’s built on is an extraordinary
amount of joint working already.

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:05.350
I think it’s helpful to reflect on the fact
that a lot of the things, for example, we

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:09.830
talked about in our public board meeting this
morning are the result of existing joint working

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:12.850
arrangements between NHSE and NHSI.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:17.310
So, this is a continuation, I think, of something
that both organisations have been striving

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:22.451
to do and putting a wrapper around it in terms
of aligning some of our structures and, perhaps

00:12:22.451 --> 00:12:28.130
more importantly, our processes and our governance
and our culture and our ways of working, which

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:31.610
perhaps haven’t had so much attention paid
as we’ve tried to make some of these programmes

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:35.190
like mental health, for example, work together.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:39.640
So, recognising that we couldn’t have got
here without all of that energy and without

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:43.800
the foundation that had already been established,
certainly before I joined NHS England six

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.460
months ago.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:51.360
And one evidence I think of that existing
foundation of joint working is when we held

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:58.200
a session with about 20 executives two weeks
ago, and we asked people to share what would

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:03.180
make this a success; how do we make sure,
after lots of experiences, perhaps of people

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:06.140
who’ve had long careers in the health service
of change programmes that perhaps haven’t

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:10.060
got exactly where they wanted to go, how do
we make sure this one does?

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:16.250
And the lessons learnt that everybody shared
in that meeting showed extraordinary commonality

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:24.560
over a huge range of experiences in the NHS:
talking about the need for a real clear purpose

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:29.440
as to why are we doing this, and then using
that purpose to drive all of the difficult

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:32.540
decisions that need to be made to make this
work.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:36.899
And that purpose, which Ian in particular’s
just spoken about, of setting ourselves up

00:13:36.899 --> 00:13:41.740
to make sure that we can provide system leadership
to the NHS in the most effective way possible

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:46.360
– I think hopefully people will recognise
this exemplified in the proposals today, but

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:50.290
also needs to be continued forward in all
of the further design work that we ned to

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:51.560
do.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:56.040
Particularly in establishing a joint culture
where – you know, when we had our board

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.200
development session yesterday, I think several
of the non executives shared views around

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:03.420
that which were extremely helpful: that we
can’t make joint working at the heart of

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:08.190
the NHS work if we aren’t clear what our
working style is, how we make decisions together

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:10.339
and have an aspiration to do that in the same
way.

00:14:10.339 --> 00:14:15.910
So, looking forward to lots more joint work
as we actually make this work for colleagues

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:18.170
and start to deliver in that spirit of joint
working.

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:19.170
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Great.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:20.170
Well, thank you very much for that.

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:25.120
I think we would add just one rider on this,
which is that the paper we’ve got today

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.520
is very much, and rightly, focused on executive
joint working.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:32.480
We’ve got to work out the governance, which
is the next layer that needs to go on top

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:33.480
of this.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:34.690
And our commitment to that – this is a job
for the NEDs, actually.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:38.000
But the commitment to that is, how do we make
this as simple as possible?

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.140
How do we get off the backs of people?

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.600
How do we get clear, sharp accountability
lines?

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:47.450
And doing things once is reflected in the
governance as well as the executive action.

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:48.709
So, we’ll take that away.

00:14:48.709 --> 00:14:49.720
We’ve made a start.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:55.000
I think the cross appointments of David Roberts,
who’s the NED on the NHSE board across the

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:59.050
NHSI as an associate non executive director,
and the reciprocal appointment of Richard

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:04.040
Douglas on the other side, has already started
to open up lines of communication and accountability

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.389
that previously were not there.

00:15:06.389 --> 00:15:11.310
So you’ve touched, Emily, particularly on
culture, and it was a subject that we dwelt

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:13.490
with on some time on yesterday.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:18.740
So, I’m just going to invite comments from
any colleagues around the boardroom table

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:22.810
on how they feel that there is a need for
cultural change.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:24.920
In other words, what have we got at the moment?

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:30.400
And then secondly, what are the main things
that we need to do as a board to drive a fresh

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.240
approach to culture across the two organisations?

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.730
Who would like to kick off on that?

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:37.730
David.

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:40.820
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thank you.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:48.470
I think all organisational mergers, whether
they be joint ventures, whether they be full

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:54.920
mergers, in my experience, and I think the
literature as well would support, succeed

00:15:54.920 --> 00:16:00.970
or fail on the creation of an appropriate
and joined up and unified culture.

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:08.480
And there are many cultures I think within
these two entities, and therefore I think

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:19.590
the challenge to Simon and to Ian is the – you,
Simon, quoted one of my leadership heroes,

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.649
Mahatma Gandhi, in: ‘We must become the
change we want to see.’

00:16:22.649 --> 00:16:29.230
And therefore, I think there is a lot of onus
on this Board – these boards, sorry – and

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:34.510
also too particularly to the two of you and
your leadership teams about working – as

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:38.500
Emily said, what are the culture, what are
the behavioural norms?

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:44.260
And of course, saying it is one thing; doing
it and living it is another which will require

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.490
real discipline.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:52.260
So, I think to me that’s as important as
the structure; it’s as important as some

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:53.260
of the other factors.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:55.860
In fact, it’s more important than those,
the behaviours.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:59.910
I think one of the most interesting paragraphs
in this paper is actually the last one, which

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:01.940
talks about the behaviours and the culture.

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:06.949
And I would encourage you, and indeed, I think
it will be incumbent on the Board to hold

00:17:06.949 --> 00:17:12.159
you to account, to create the right – to
come back to us with your thoughts and ideas

00:17:12.159 --> 00:17:14.240
about what is the target culture.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:20.970
But to Emily’s point as well, I was privileged
to be in that room when we had that conversation.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:26.089
I was mighty struck by the fact that this
is, I think, a very fundamental change between

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:35.179
– previously it was a top down reorganisation
to sort of try and drive a performance into

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:38.179
the people who are most important people,
are the people who are looking after our people

00:17:38.179 --> 00:17:39.470
and our patients.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:44.190
This is actually fundamentally different;
this is about a reorganisation in order to

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:47.470
serve the people who are – and the patients.

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:52.009
Because it’s about us recognising that the
world has changed, and that we need to change

00:17:52.009 --> 00:17:57.190
the way we operate in order to facilitate
change and better outcomes for people and

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:58.190
for patients.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:01.850
That’s a very fundamental change in the
way we operate, and in the culture we will

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:04.169
need to have, which will test all of us.

00:18:04.169 --> 00:18:10.080
So it’s a good challenge for both of you.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:11.669
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, David.

00:18:11.669 --> 00:18:12.669
Other comments?

00:18:12.669 --> 00:18:13.669
Moira.

00:18:13.669 --> 00:18:18.340
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Malcolm, it’s not a comment
about the cultures, because lots of people

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.359
in this room will be attending to that.

00:18:20.359 --> 00:18:26.609
But I just wanted to acknowledge the very
hard work that our executive colleagues have

00:18:26.609 --> 00:18:28.880
put in to getting us to this place.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:34.399
I’m sure it has taken a lot of their time
and attention.

00:18:34.399 --> 00:18:39.159
And to pay tribute to both chairs, actually,
for clearly enabling that.

00:18:39.159 --> 00:18:42.029
Without your support they wouldn’t have
been able to do that work.

00:18:42.029 --> 00:18:46.330
But of course, it is only the very first step.

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:51.739
And I think the risk is in, as David talked
to, any of these kinds of coming-together

00:18:51.739 --> 00:18:56.159
that the energy and focus turns inwards.

00:18:56.159 --> 00:19:03.539
And there is so much at stake in this NHS
of ours in its 70th year that you can’t

00:19:03.539 --> 00:19:05.059
allow that to happen.

00:19:05.059 --> 00:19:10.970
But it will be very hard to do both the internal
stuff and the external stuff.

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:15.820
So, everything that they’ve already done
will appear easy in comparison to the next

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:16.820
stages.

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:20.249
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: The first test of this is
going to be the next two items on the agenda.

00:19:20.249 --> 00:19:27.929
So let me invite any further comments on this
paper before we go onto other items of business.

00:19:27.929 --> 00:19:28.929
Victor?

00:19:28.929 --> 00:19:33.570
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Just a reflection on the
things that might bind us together that we’re

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:38.690
going to have to do work on, that we’ve
made some progress certainly in rhetoric if

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:40.859
not in – the first is diversity.

00:19:40.859 --> 00:19:47.299
I don’t just mean diversity – because
whenever the black guy says this, everybody

00:19:47.299 --> 00:19:49.429
thinks it’s about race; it’s about diversity
of thought.

00:19:49.429 --> 00:19:57.750
To David’s point, the future’s created
by the things we don’t talk about, and that’s

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:02.419
often enhanced if there are different – so,
I’m wondering about how we’re developing

00:20:02.419 --> 00:20:11.019
those themes that are cross-cutting but need
to be developed in the governance and the

00:20:11.019 --> 00:20:13.769
leadership, because they will be reflected
in what they do.

00:20:13.769 --> 00:20:19.479
And the other, given this morning’s conversation
in which we touched on themes of inequality,

00:20:19.479 --> 00:20:26.610
the extent to which these themes start here
and are driven through the joint work of both

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:34.429
boards, into how we assess programmes, strategies,
operations, and impact on the people out there

00:20:34.429 --> 00:20:36.309
who are paying for all this.

00:20:36.309 --> 00:20:39.840
I’m just curious about how we address that.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:48.320
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: So Victor, that gives me
an opportunity to also just set out perhaps

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:55.749
in brief detail the proposition in the paper
around the creation of an NHS assembly, which

00:20:55.749 --> 00:21:03.159
is going to be a national space where the
stakeholders – both patient groups, voluntary

00:21:03.159 --> 00:21:09.549
and community sector, clinical groups, as
well as front-line leaders drawn from across

00:21:09.549 --> 00:21:15.599
different sectors of local government and
the NHS – come together, both to take stock

00:21:15.599 --> 00:21:20.409
on where the NHS has got to, but also to help
co-design what the next ten years will look

00:21:20.409 --> 00:21:21.409
like.

00:21:21.409 --> 00:21:25.929
And frankly, that will hold a mirror up to
how we’re doing and provide another forum

00:21:25.929 --> 00:21:29.940
for challenge to keep us on the true path.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:39.539
And we aim to have the NHS assembly convened
shortly before – or shortly after the 70th

00:21:39.539 --> 00:21:41.840
anniversary on 5th July.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.200
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Really good.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:45.200
Sigurd?

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:51.279
&gt;&gt;Sigurd Reinton: Just some observations really,
as much as anything else.

00:21:51.279 --> 00:21:57.429
I think as we all discussed informally yesterday,
it’s quite clear that this is not only something

00:21:57.429 --> 00:22:01.159
we need to do and ought to do, but we probably
ought to have done some time ago.

00:22:01.159 --> 00:22:03.769
But better late than never.

00:22:03.769 --> 00:22:10.519
The observations are really to do with – first
of all, we mustn’t underestimate the magnitude

00:22:10.519 --> 00:22:13.299
of the cultural change that’s involved here.

00:22:13.299 --> 00:22:19.309
And that is I think particularly true when
we look at the way we interact with our charges,

00:22:19.309 --> 00:22:22.270
if I can call them that, out in the regions.

00:22:22.270 --> 00:22:27.359
And that’s going to be quite important to
bear in mind, because it will unfortunately

00:22:27.359 --> 00:22:31.989
– and in a way this will be very difficult
– mean that a lot of people whose skills

00:22:31.989 --> 00:22:36.679
were appropriate to the roles we played in
the past may or may not be appropriate in

00:22:36.679 --> 00:22:37.679
the future.

00:22:37.679 --> 00:22:42.450
And that will be for Ian and Simon a really
difficult thing.

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:48.710
The other observation that I wanted to share
is that one of the few really important levers,

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:52.419
apart from improved leadership that we’ve
talked about, is obviously to really push

00:22:52.419 --> 00:22:57.419
the digital agenda, and we must avail ourselves
of that to the fullest possible extent.

00:22:57.419 --> 00:23:05.100
There are not many ways that you can square
the circle of rising demand and finite funds,

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:10.220
even if we do get a little bit more now, but
digital and pushing that in every sense – both

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:15.309
to run our own affairs more efficiently, but
also improving patient care – is clearly

00:23:15.309 --> 00:23:16.679
one of them.

00:23:16.679 --> 00:23:23.929
And then finally, we will have a very interesting
job of working out how we do improvement on

00:23:23.929 --> 00:23:30.899
the one hand, and on the other hand, intervene
when local health economies are in trouble.

00:23:30.899 --> 00:23:35.700
And again, we need to pay, I think, quite
careful attention to how we organise to do

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:36.700
that.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:37.700
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Sigurd.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:42.299
It should be of no surprise to anybody that
at our public board meeting this morning,

00:23:42.299 --> 00:23:47.980
the answer to most of our impossible challenges
was: we’ll do it with NHS Improvement.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:55.390
That was true of maternity transformation,
health inequalities and mental health.

00:23:55.390 --> 00:23:57.809
And actually, I think there’s a strong earnest[?]
of intention.

00:23:57.809 --> 00:24:01.409
We’re very fortunate of course, to have
the Chair of NHS Digital on the Board, Noel.

00:24:01.409 --> 00:24:03.240
And I think you might want to come in at this
point.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:04.789
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I did, Malcolm, yes.

00:24:04.789 --> 00:24:09.330
Not specifically on the digital issue, but
it relates.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:12.219
Culture is important, but so is style.

00:24:12.219 --> 00:24:18.840
And what I mean by style is, apart from joining
up organisationally and structurally, I think

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:22.330
it’s equally important when we meet that
we join up our thinking.

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:29.620
And typically, we see things historically
that have come through a very programmatic

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:31.619
lens.

00:24:31.619 --> 00:24:37.480
And increasingly, I think we have the opportunity
as we start relatively new to look at issues

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:46.200
through a series of matrix lenses: through
workforce, through technology, through inequalities,

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:50.279
through what we do with patient and public
voice.

00:24:50.279 --> 00:24:56.169
And actually, I would really like to see one
change above all in the way that we debate

00:24:56.169 --> 00:25:03.889
and decide on issues: is that we get the multidisciplinary
view of the problems that we have and the

00:25:03.889 --> 00:25:09.409
challenges that we have, as opposed to the
classical programmatic or institutionally

00:25:09.409 --> 00:25:11.399
siloed view.

00:25:11.399 --> 00:25:17.179
Because joining up the thinking is in a way
even more important, or at least emblematic

00:25:17.179 --> 00:25:19.129
of how we’re going to join up the culture.

00:25:19.129 --> 00:25:25.509
And I think that style could also be a fresh
start for many of the – for the way we look

00:25:25.509 --> 00:25:26.509
at many things.

00:25:26.509 --> 00:25:27.509
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thanks, Noel.

00:25:27.509 --> 00:25:30.940
I’m going to bring in Richard, and then
I think we need to move on in the agenda.

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:31.940
Richard?

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:33.149
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Just a couple of quick
points.

00:25:33.149 --> 00:25:38.820
First of all, this is a fantastic opportunity,
and one of the reasons it’s a fantastic

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:43.690
opportunity is this is something we’ve designed
ourselves, we’re doing because it’s the

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:45.259
right thing to do.

00:25:45.259 --> 00:25:48.629
And too many people around the room maybe
have been scarred by other change programmes,

00:25:48.629 --> 00:25:51.619
where you’re implementing someone else’s
template.

00:25:51.619 --> 00:25:57.529
But with that comes – without trying to
quote Spiderman, but comes a degree of responsibility

00:25:57.529 --> 00:26:02.809
around how we do it, because it does belong
– it does belong to us.

00:26:02.809 --> 00:26:07.580
I just think we have got to really focus on
the fact: this is ours, we design it, we own

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:08.580
it.

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:11.419
But linked with that as well, linked to the
question it’s not being imposed upon us:

00:26:11.419 --> 00:26:17.629
when we think about how we want to change
ways of working and change in culture in the

00:26:17.629 --> 00:26:23.789
organisation, we’ve also got to be really
careful of not being – dissing the past

00:26:23.789 --> 00:26:24.789
in some way.

00:26:24.789 --> 00:26:29.200
Because the cultures and the ways of working
were there for a reason, they worked in that

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:33.380
time for the things that people wanted to
do.

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:38.740
And we’re changing because the world around
us has changed, the things we’re trying

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:40.359
to do have changed.

00:26:40.359 --> 00:26:43.559
And I just think when we deal, particularly
with our own people on this, we’ve got to

00:26:43.559 --> 00:26:47.690
be quite clear it’s not because the past
was a bad place; it’s because the world

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:48.690
has changed.

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:52.580
And I think we’ve got to hold that all the
time when we go through this work.

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:53.809
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good.

00:26:53.809 --> 00:26:59.549
Well, thank you very much Richard, and thank
you all board members who’ve contributed.

00:26:59.549 --> 00:27:05.840
Before I invite Dido to assume the chair,
I would just like to say that this is a very

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:08.080
big adventure that we’re going on.

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:09.210
It’s really complicated.

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:12.899
There’s an enormous amount of work that’s
gone into it already, and it is today only

00:27:12.899 --> 00:27:16.479
the starting point of a significant change
programme.

00:27:16.479 --> 00:27:20.609
Emily and Ben, I know will do an enormous
amount now to take forward on the next steps.

00:27:20.609 --> 00:27:26.330
But it would be wrong of me to close this
item on the agenda without paying huge tribute

00:27:26.330 --> 00:27:28.259
to our two Chief Executives.

00:27:28.259 --> 00:27:32.869
This hasn’t been easy; I mean, this has
involved an enormous amount – not hand-to-hand

00:27:32.869 --> 00:27:35.389
conflict, that would be completely the wrong
way of characterising.

00:27:35.389 --> 00:27:36.389
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Handholding?

00:27:36.389 --> 00:27:37.389
Mutual handholding?

00:27:37.389 --> 00:27:39.009
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes, yes, hip welding and
handholding.

00:27:39.009 --> 00:27:40.009
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Walking together.

00:27:40.009 --> 00:27:41.009
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Too many scary analogies.

00:27:41.009 --> 00:27:45.659
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: But you know, not to trivialise
it, they are the ones who have taken the lead

00:27:45.659 --> 00:27:49.309
and those are the ones who will bear the lead
and the responsibility for the future.

00:27:49.309 --> 00:27:53.399
What you both need to know is actually the
boards are completely joined up behind this.

00:27:53.399 --> 00:27:58.419
You will have the support of the boards, and
we will not only hold you to account but also

00:27:58.419 --> 00:28:03.210
help you to drive the changes with our staff
and others to make this a success.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:07.019
So, on that basis may I invite you, Dido,
to assume the chair?

00:28:07.019 --> 00:28:08.179
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thanks, Malcolm.

00:28:08.179 --> 00:28:13.320
I’d just like to add one comment which is
I think that, to echo Malcolm’s, the way

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:17.339
we do this is, if anything, more important
than what we do.

00:28:17.339 --> 00:28:21.979
And I think the way the two teams, our two
CEOs and all of the executives working on

00:28:21.979 --> 00:28:26.489
this have been working on this is exemplary,
and we need to keep doing that.

00:28:26.489 --> 00:28:32.099
So I think that in the back end of the paper,
project 70, our approach to really listening

00:28:32.099 --> 00:28:39.749
and engaging with our teams to make sure that
we take the best of both worlds and make something

00:28:39.749 --> 00:28:45.429
even better, both for the people working in
NHS Improvement and NHS England and the citizens

00:28:45.429 --> 00:28:49.960
of the country, and the staff of the NHS more
broadly, is really important.

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:52.840
And I think you’ve made a very, very, good
start at that.

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:56.940
And I think both of you said to each of us
separately, to Malcolm and I separately, that

00:28:56.940 --> 00:29:01.099
the more you talked, the more you discovered
that you agreed with each other and the more

00:29:01.099 --> 00:29:03.179
opportunity there was to work together.

00:29:03.179 --> 00:29:06.099
And I just think we’ll find that going forward
even more.

00:29:06.099 --> 00:29:11.470
So with that, we need to do – as Simon said,
we need to look out, not in.

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:17.999
And we get that opportunity now, to switch
focus and look at where we stand together.

00:29:17.999 --> 00:29:24.899
And this really is the beginning of the new
world of our joint view of where we stand

00:29:24.899 --> 00:29:27.049
in terms of the planning refresh for 18/19.

00:29:27.049 --> 00:29:30.779
Paul and Elizabeth, are you both going to
give us a bit of summary?

00:29:30.779 --> 00:29:32.309
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: We are indeed.

00:29:32.309 --> 00:29:36.149
We thought we’d start with a nice easy topic
for our joint work together, so sorting out

00:29:36.149 --> 00:29:39.409
the conundrum of 18/19 seems like it.

00:29:39.409 --> 00:29:43.599
To manage expectations, this is a progress
update that Elizabeth and I are going to give

00:29:43.599 --> 00:29:46.979
on the financial and operational planning
exercise.

00:29:46.979 --> 00:29:50.679
Although it is getting towards the end of
May, this is not a completed exercise, but

00:29:50.679 --> 00:29:55.080
we think it’s very important that both boards
have a good understanding of where we’re

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:56.099
at, at the moment.

00:29:56.099 --> 00:29:59.210
In the context, Dido, of the discussion that
we’ve just had, it’s worth pointing out

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:05.269
that this planning process itself has already
been a good example of joint working in action,

00:30:05.269 --> 00:30:08.879
with everything from the creation of the planning
guidance through to the setting of priorities

00:30:08.879 --> 00:30:13.570
for the system, and now the agreement of plans
with local systems, being very much undertaken

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:14.570
together.

00:30:14.570 --> 00:30:18.889
And although this particular presentation
of the state of affairs is being led from

00:30:18.889 --> 00:30:25.599
the bean-counting corner, if I can call us
that, it has very much been a multifunctional,

00:30:25.599 --> 00:30:30.179
multidisciplinary process involving operational
colleagues and clinical colleagues.

00:30:30.179 --> 00:30:34.869
So it needs to be seen in that context, because
we can’t solve this from one disciplinary

00:30:34.869 --> 00:30:38.009
lens any more than we can from one organisational
lens.

00:30:38.009 --> 00:30:42.299
I’m just going to give the briefest of introductions,
and then with Elizabeth we’re going to kind

00:30:42.299 --> 00:30:45.149
of give you the update on the various dimensions
of it.

00:30:45.149 --> 00:30:50.859
The paper at paragraphs 9 and 10 reminds the
Board of the priorities we set for the deployment

00:30:50.859 --> 00:30:56.119
of the extra £2.1 billion that were made
available to the NHS following the Autumn

00:30:56.119 --> 00:31:01.200
Budget, and the specific challenge that we
set for each local system to work together

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:04.399
to create credible and aligned plans.

00:31:04.399 --> 00:31:08.209
So the boards might want to have that in front
of them.

00:31:08.209 --> 00:31:11.899
And essentially, the rigorous assessment process
which Elizabeth and I and our operational

00:31:11.899 --> 00:31:15.969
colleagues in particular have been undertaking
together, following the planned submissions

00:31:15.969 --> 00:31:21.659
at the end of April, has been very much focused
around the six key criteria for success, which

00:31:21.659 --> 00:31:25.669
again I’d like the boards to have in mind
as we take us through this.

00:31:25.669 --> 00:31:30.239
The first of which, of course, is that we
need organisations to create plans which show

00:31:30.239 --> 00:31:35.789
how they’re going to deliver their part
of the financial balance for the system which

00:31:35.789 --> 00:31:40.739
we are committed to, incorporating the extra
funding that we’ve given through the provider

00:31:40.739 --> 00:31:46.469
sustainability fund, topped up as we know
to £2.45 billion, and the new commissioner

00:31:46.469 --> 00:31:47.499
sustainability fund.

00:31:47.499 --> 00:31:51.739
So question number one is: have we got to
financial balance in both the provider sector

00:31:51.739 --> 00:31:57.059
and the commissioner sector, through individual
organisations and systems meeting the expectations

00:31:57.059 --> 00:32:00.220
that we have set with them and for them?

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:05.289
The second clear requirement is that we’ve
got sufficient mitigation in place for the

00:32:05.289 --> 00:32:10.370
significant risks, which every year in any
decent planning process all organisations

00:32:10.370 --> 00:32:11.929
will identify for themselves.

00:32:11.929 --> 00:32:14.889
So, that’s the second test we’ve been
setting.

00:32:14.889 --> 00:32:20.080
And I would just remind the boards that that’s
even more important this year because we haven’t

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:25.580
got the cushion of a system reserve set aside
at the beginning of the year to cover risks

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:27.339
as they emerge during the course of the year.

00:32:27.339 --> 00:32:33.240
We’ve put all of that money into the available
funds for organisations to put behind, in

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:37.419
particular, the activity plans that they’ve
generated, so it’s really important that

00:32:37.419 --> 00:32:40.769
we’ve got a sound handle on the risk as
we go through it.

00:32:40.769 --> 00:32:41.962
So that’s the second test.

00:32:41.962 --> 00:32:46.779
The third one is we are determined to have
plausible activity growth in the plans for

00:32:46.779 --> 00:32:50.019
17 – for 18/19; that’s a Freudian slip.

00:32:50.019 --> 00:32:54.359
Learning from, frankly, some of the issues
that we’ve had in 17/18 where activity planning

00:32:54.359 --> 00:32:57.090
was not up to scratch at the beginning of
the year.

00:32:57.090 --> 00:33:03.669
We need to start this year with credible activity
plans, with a particular focus on emergency

00:33:03.669 --> 00:33:10.629
activity, which clearly was the big challenge
for us in 17/18, but with an eye also to delivering

00:33:10.629 --> 00:33:13.600
on the commitments we’ve made around RTT.

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:17.669
The fourth is, it’s all very well to have
activity plans and financial plans which deliver

00:33:17.669 --> 00:33:21.429
all that’s needed: we need to make sure
that organisations have got the operational

00:33:21.429 --> 00:33:27.499
capacity – in particular, beds and workforce
– to deliver the activity that we’re planning

00:33:27.499 --> 00:33:28.629
for.

00:33:28.629 --> 00:33:34.269
Fifthly, we need to make sure that we achieve
the holy grail in the NHS, which even in Richard’s

00:33:34.269 --> 00:33:39.820
time looking after the central finances we
never quite cracked, which was to have commissioners

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:45.450
and providers planning for the same amount
of cost and income in an entirely consistent

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:46.450
way.

00:33:46.450 --> 00:33:49.639
We are determined to have that sorted out
this year.

00:33:49.639 --> 00:33:53.989
And finally, we set some very clear expectations
of commissioners with regard to investment

00:33:53.989 --> 00:33:58.070
in key priority areas, and I would single
out particularly mental health and primary

00:33:58.070 --> 00:33:59.070
care.

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:02.999
So, those six lenses are the way in which
we’ve been looking at the planning process,

00:34:02.999 --> 00:34:07.059
and which now we’re going to give you a
very brief kind of non numerical update.

00:34:07.059 --> 00:34:12.750
Clearly, when we’ve finished the process,
we will furnish the set of numbers which deliver

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:14.869
on those six things.

00:34:14.869 --> 00:34:20.649
The good news is that the April submissions
that we had from commissioners and providers

00:34:20.649 --> 00:34:25.039
were a substantial improvement on earlier
draft plans that the boards will independently

00:34:25.039 --> 00:34:31.879
have seen, and all but about 30 organisations,
concentrated in 15 of our most challenged

00:34:31.879 --> 00:34:36.139
health economies, have now confirmed in their
plans that they’ll deliver the required

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:38.109
financial performance.

00:34:38.109 --> 00:34:41.760
The bad news is that some of the exceptions
are pretty big.

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:46.510
So over the next few weeks, the real focus
for financial balancing is to be working with

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:53.539
those health economies to work out what set
of actions will get the gaps in their plans

00:34:53.539 --> 00:34:57.480
to be eliminated in ways that they can deliver.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:35:02.350
It’s already evident from the work that
Elizabeth and I have done with the regional

00:35:02.350 --> 00:35:05.910
teams that in quite a number of those places,
some really difficult choices are going to

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:10.750
have to be made in order to close the gaps
that they are showing in their latest set

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:11.880
of plans.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:17.530
And it will be a very important test, I think,
of our shared endeavour, both nationally and

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:23.579
regionally, as to how, as we go through those,
in particular the top 15 health economies,

00:35:23.579 --> 00:35:29.720
how we can manage to galvanise action to make
the right difficult choices, in order to get

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:31.049
all of that sorted out.

00:35:31.049 --> 00:35:33.589
So, that’s the financial balancing.

00:35:33.589 --> 00:35:37.019
We’ll come back to all this in discussion,
I dare say.

00:35:37.019 --> 00:35:42.029
The level of risk reported alongside the plans
is significantly lower than in previous years.

00:35:42.029 --> 00:35:46.849
That’s an important factor I think in all
of this, but it’s still higher than it needs

00:35:46.849 --> 00:35:51.240
to be to give us the assurance that I talked
about earlier on.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:56.390
It mainly reflects, as is often each case,
cost improvement plans on both commissioners

00:35:56.390 --> 00:36:02.200
and providers, which have not yet been sufficiently
well identified and underpinned with credible

00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:03.540
actions.

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:08.530
That was the case last year, and I’m afraid
that on the whole, risks that we identified

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:13.589
at the beginning of the year with unidentified
SIP plans translated by the end of the year

00:36:13.589 --> 00:36:16.230
into overspends on the part of the organisations
concerned.

00:36:16.230 --> 00:36:21.819
So it’s vitally important that in the next
three or four weeks we do identify and then

00:36:21.819 --> 00:36:26.680
underpin the SIP plans and the QIP plans,
in our wonderful terminology; the efficiency

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:31.650
plans of commissioners and providers so that
we don’t have all of that falling out at

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:35.070
the back end of the year as under-delivery.

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:36.070
So that’s the SIP plans.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:40.329
Did you want to comment at all on SIP plans
and how we’re working on that, Elizabeth?

00:36:40.329 --> 00:36:44.440
&gt;&gt;Elizabeth O’Mahony: Yes, so in addition
to the points that you raised in terms of

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:50.600
the tests, Paul, I know it was inherent in
what you said, but another key test obviously

00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:57.369
that is at the very front of our minds at
the moment is the potential impact of quality

00:36:57.369 --> 00:36:58.369
on the plans.

00:36:58.369 --> 00:36:59.440
I think that’s really important.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:04.590
So, this is in respect of the extent in which
constitutional standards are met, but also

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:10.000
in respect of the quality impact assessment
of the SIPs and QIPs that are coming through.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:11.000
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Exactly, yes.

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:14.049
&gt;&gt;Elizabeth O’Mahony: So, jointly across
the teams with clinical colleagues, we are

00:37:14.049 --> 00:37:22.049
working hard at it to sense-check the plans,
but also with clinical colleagues out with

00:37:22.049 --> 00:37:27.940
the providers, to understand the ramifications
of the savings plans that are coming through.

00:37:27.940 --> 00:37:32.140
So once again, we’re seeing quite high numbers;
we’re trying to assess the risk of those

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:34.519
and what those mean.

00:37:34.519 --> 00:37:40.570
When we’re looking at the SIPs plans, we’re
also looking at the quality and the assumptions

00:37:40.570 --> 00:37:43.140
made about capacity planning.

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:48.539
And that, again – so it’s great that the
plans are more aligned; we need to make sure

00:37:48.539 --> 00:37:50.940
that they are realistic.

00:37:50.940 --> 00:37:57.619
And as we currently stand, at a very top-down
modelling approach it looks as though we need

00:37:57.619 --> 00:37:58.920
the extra capacity.

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:02.530
And with that, working with clinical colleagues
again, we are concerned about whether or not

00:38:02.530 --> 00:38:04.650
the workforce actually exists.

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:09.610
Or that might be appropriate, and we need
to understand how perhaps we harness some

00:38:09.610 --> 00:38:15.240
of the length-of-stay initiatives and other
things that we know that are out there, and

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:21.609
with our productivity colleagues we know that
they’re out there to capitalise on.

00:38:21.609 --> 00:38:25.280
So, lots of work going on, a huge amount of
joint work.

00:38:25.280 --> 00:38:29.359
I’d just like to echo that this is not the
start, as Paul said, of the joint working;

00:38:29.359 --> 00:38:36.910
this has been going on since the autumn probably,
and it’s been fantastic particularly how

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:40.559
the planning teams have worked together, regularly
meeting.

00:38:40.559 --> 00:38:45.990
But also the regions, so at a regional level
we’ve never been at this stage of a planning

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:52.640
round where we’ve had such tight and close
understanding of the planning gaps and the

00:38:52.640 --> 00:38:54.450
contract gaps.

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:58.080
And that’s really positive, that even if
there is still a gap at this stage of the

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:01.250
year, we actually understand the level of
risk collectively.

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:04.710
So that’s very, very positive from previous
years.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:05.710
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Yes.

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:10.660
So, if I may round off the six tests: we’ve
talked about balancing, we’ve talked about

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:15.829
SIPs and QIPs, both in quality terms and in
terms of identification and confidence in

00:39:15.829 --> 00:39:16.829
delivery.

00:39:16.829 --> 00:39:18.059
The third one was activity.

00:39:18.059 --> 00:39:24.170
The good news on activity is that the systems
have come back with plans which respond directly

00:39:24.170 --> 00:39:32.050
to the challenge that we set on non elective
volumes, so we asked systems to plan for 2.3%

00:39:32.050 --> 00:39:35.579
of growth in activity in aggregate across
the country.

00:39:35.579 --> 00:39:40.819
The good news is both commissioner plans and
provider plans at national level, and in general

00:39:40.819 --> 00:39:44.190
across the country, have come back with almost
exactly that number.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:49.940
So the system is planning for a much more
sensible level of non elective activity than

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:52.279
was the case in 17/18.

00:39:52.279 --> 00:39:57.150
The more challenging part of it is whether
the level of elective activity that’s been

00:39:57.150 --> 00:40:00.650
planned for, particularly by commissioners
– there is a difference between commissioner

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:05.480
and provider plans at the moment on elective
volume – whether either of those sets of

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:09.550
volumes actually is sufficient to deliver
on the commitment that we’ve made that waiting

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:14.140
lists will be, at worst, stabilised.

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:18.190
And that’s the piece of testing that we
will need to do in the context of real expectations

00:40:18.190 --> 00:40:19.690
over the next few weeks.

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:22.690
I think you’ve referred to the fourth test,
which is capacity planning.

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:23.690
&gt;&gt;Elizabeth O’Mahony: Capacity, yes.

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:24.690
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Have you covered that sufficiently?

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:25.690
&gt;&gt;Elizabeth O’Mahony: Yes, I have.

00:40:25.690 --> 00:40:26.690
Yes.

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:29.410
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: So, that’s a very big part
of what we’re doing over the next few weeks.

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:34.210
On cross-sector alignment, which as I say
is the holy grail which we are attempting

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:38.770
to do, the great news is we’ve got much
greater transparency on this this year than

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:43.099
we’ve ever had before, helped greatly by
the fact that STPs have taken on this particular

00:40:43.099 --> 00:40:45.539
challenge to bring all this together.

00:40:45.539 --> 00:40:50.880
With transparency, of course, comes knowledge
which we therefore need to respond to.

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:54.390
The good news is, whilst there is still a
gap, it is a much smaller gap than we think

00:40:54.390 --> 00:40:59.180
we’ve ever had before, which again speaks
I think to the consistency of the planning

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:00.180
around activity.

00:41:00.180 --> 00:41:03.930
So, we have still got to wrestle down some
small gaps.

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:08.880
Something well over 90% of the country has
now got agreed contracts, which is a good

00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:13.829
piece of development, and there is probably
a handful of places that have got serious

00:41:13.829 --> 00:41:18.730
issues still with their contracts, where we’re
going to need to support them to come to conclusions

00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:19.730
on that.

00:41:19.730 --> 00:41:23.849
So, I think good progress being made but we
still have a bit of work to do.

00:41:23.849 --> 00:41:27.450
And the final thing is a small piece of good
news, which is unusual for finance people

00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:31.020
to bring in any board meeting, but let me
break the habit of a lifetime.

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:36.750
So, mental health investment standards and
primary care investment – the two big token

00:41:36.750 --> 00:41:41.650
– not token; totemic investments that we
were very keen – I’m not doing the good

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:44.630
news very well, am I?

00:41:44.630 --> 00:41:50.590
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: When finance directors try
to give good news!

00:41:50.590 --> 00:41:57.819
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: So, the very important investments
which we required people to make are secured

00:41:57.819 --> 00:41:59.160
within the plans.

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:03.480
There is not a single CCG in the country that
has not fulfilled – that’s a double negative

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:07.400
if ever there was one – the mental health
investment standard, and every single CCG

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:13.609
is investing the right amount of money in
the additional investments behind GP access

00:42:13.609 --> 00:42:15.119
and scaled-up working.

00:42:15.119 --> 00:42:19.430
So, we’ve got at least those investments
sorted out, as well as, as I say, a large

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:22.140
part of the activity conundrum solved.

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:29.329
So, in that attempt to bring this discussion
to a – or at least our presentation of it,

00:42:29.329 --> 00:42:36.839
to a positive end, I guess we open to questions,
observations and contributions, financial

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:40.100
or otherwise, from around the table to the
balancing act.

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:41.240
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you both.

00:42:44.140 --> 00:42:45.600
Sarah, would you like to kick us off?

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:52.700
&gt;&gt;Sarah Harkness: First of all, glad to hear
there’s some good news and that gaps are

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:54.740
closing, which is what we like to hear.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:43:00.560
I hope that, to the extent that that’s the
result of closer working out in the regions,

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:07.079
out in the localities, the finance staff out
in those places are finding that a good experience.

00:43:07.079 --> 00:43:11.130
Because the closer they work together – obviously,
we want to know that everything that we’re

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:14.160
doing is going to make things better out on
the front line, so that’s good.

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:20.869
The question I had was whether the plans for
A&amp;E attendance growth, non elective growth,

00:43:20.869 --> 00:43:24.920
whether you were still comfortable with those
numbers now we’ve had the winter?

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:28.180
Because of course, those assumptions will
have been set at the beginning of the winter,

00:43:28.180 --> 00:43:30.079
we’ve been through a very challenging winter.

00:43:30.079 --> 00:43:31.130
Are they still the right assumptions?

00:43:31.130 --> 00:43:37.890
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Elizabeth might have a view;
indeed, other colleagues, Matthew and others

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:40.000
might have a view on that.

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:44.950
It is undoubtedly the case that since we set
the benchmark for the planning, the growth

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:48.529
for last year was higher than the 2.3% that
we set.

00:43:48.529 --> 00:43:53.180
I think 3.4% is roughly speaking where we
end up for the whole of last year.

00:43:53.180 --> 00:43:57.640
And I think the judgement we have to make
is whether there were sufficient particular

00:43:57.640 --> 00:44:03.900
factors in 17/18 that lead us to think that
we will still be okay with 2.3%.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:08.789
I can point to the flu incident obviously
as being a specific.

00:44:08.789 --> 00:44:12.970
We can think of some of the aspects of winter
and the length of winter that we had as being

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:14.270
specific.

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:18.890
And I can think of one or two aspects of the,
if you like, recording of activity, which

00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:23.569
I think will lead us to have a slightly overstated
view as to what the trend is.

00:44:23.569 --> 00:44:28.830
There is undoubtedly a risk that 2.3% will
not be quite enough for the winter.

00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:34.400
I think we’re an awful lot better off though,
having secured 2.3% of activity in the plans,

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:38.299
than having something which in previous years
was rather more optimistic than that.

00:44:38.299 --> 00:44:39.299
&gt;&gt;Elizabeth O’Mahony: Yeah.

00:44:39.299 --> 00:44:43.510
Could I just add the other thing that is very
positive this year is the profiling of activity;

00:44:43.510 --> 00:44:46.680
so, it does look more realistic.

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:51.450
Within this, everything – so, not to caveat
the good news – within this, we’ve got

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:52.450
variation.

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:56.500
So, we’ve got some very good plans and we’re
in a much better place, but we’ve still

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:59.319
got further work to do across this.

00:44:59.319 --> 00:45:04.819
So, a very good set of plans, but on winter,
I think we’ve obviously started the winter

00:45:04.819 --> 00:45:08.800
planning as well earlier in the process, with
the help of Pauline.

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:11.570
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ruth, you wanted to come in?

00:45:11.570 --> 00:45:12.609
And then Matthew.

00:45:12.609 --> 00:45:15.849
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Just wanted to go back to this
being more than just a finance function.

00:45:15.849 --> 00:45:18.799
And so thank you for recognising that.

00:45:18.799 --> 00:45:23.990
Because we as clinical colleagues have had
to have a discussion as well along with you

00:45:23.990 --> 00:45:30.340
about how we support our clinical colleagues
out in the provider sector, and I’m sure

00:45:30.340 --> 00:45:34.180
in the commissioning sector, with their quality
impact assessments.

00:45:34.180 --> 00:45:37.720
And at the ‘star chambers’ that we’re
sort of halfway through at the moment, we’ve

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:43.589
been thinking about how we support our providers,
whether that’s around workforce or whether

00:45:43.589 --> 00:45:45.040
that’s around quality impact.

00:45:45.040 --> 00:45:49.940
So together, I think this greater alignment
will allow us to do that even more focused,

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:53.020
so a broader benefit for the front line directors.

00:45:53.020 --> 00:45:54.210
It’s really important stuff.

00:45:54.210 --> 00:45:55.210
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Matthew?

00:45:55.210 --> 00:46:00.250
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I just wanted to come
back to the numbers point, because we did

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:03.900
– seeing that the growth was higher than
the end of the year, we did go back around

00:46:03.900 --> 00:46:07.222
the regional directors and say to them that
when they’re assessing the plans, they need

00:46:07.222 --> 00:46:08.780
to bear that in mind.

00:46:08.780 --> 00:46:12.779
And looking at the numbers that have come
through, if you look at the overnight stay

00:46:12.779 --> 00:46:17.750
emergency admissions, we asked people to plan
against 0.9% growth and they’ve actually

00:46:17.750 --> 00:46:18.930
come back with 1.3%.

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:23.740
So, they’ve come in higher than the numbers
that we sent out for the overnight stay ones.

00:46:23.740 --> 00:46:28.029
So that would suggest that locally, although
you can never be sure that every single place

00:46:28.029 --> 00:46:33.010
has done it rigorously, on average locally
they’ve come in with numbers that are higher

00:46:33.010 --> 00:46:35.920
growth numbers than the planning guidance
we gave them.

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:37.390
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay?

00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:38.390
Ian?

00:46:38.390 --> 00:46:39.390
Simon?

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:40.390
Sorry, Richard first and then [inaudible].

00:46:40.390 --> 00:46:41.799
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: It’s only a quick one.

00:46:41.799 --> 00:46:46.900
I mean, the two key tests I guess for me looking
at this are that the plans are realistic and

00:46:46.900 --> 00:46:50.559
aligned, which I think were the two words
that you used, Paul.

00:46:50.559 --> 00:46:56.369
This feels a lot more aligned than it was
this time last year.

00:46:56.369 --> 00:46:59.970
So you reduce some of the risk by better alignment.

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:06.240
I guess the thing to really push back on as
we go back to organisations is the individual

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.019
realism as well.

00:47:08.019 --> 00:47:12.569
So alignment, fantastic, reduces risk.

00:47:12.569 --> 00:47:17.259
But we’ve really got to keep pressing back
on how realistic are the assumptions, so we

00:47:17.259 --> 00:47:21.279
don’t end up getting any surprises later
on in the year.

00:47:21.279 --> 00:47:25.220
So, that’s the only part I’d say; that’s
the bit to keep pursuing in all of this.

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:27.910
&gt;&gt;Elizabeth Mahony: So, that’s the next
stage of the adherence process that we’re

00:47:27.910 --> 00:47:29.170
following for providers.

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:34.690
And in particular for the acute providers,
we will be writing out to chairs to confirm

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:39.329
our sense check of the plans, and to make
sure that the assumptions we are making before

00:47:39.329 --> 00:47:45.640
we accept the plans, we understand, and we
understand the wider ramifications of those.

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:49.069
And I think that’s really important that
we do that stage in the process that we haven’t

00:47:49.069 --> 00:47:50.069
done previously.

00:47:50.069 --> 00:47:51.069
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Agreed.

00:47:51.069 --> 00:47:53.319
Ian, Simon, would you like to close out?

00:47:53.319 --> 00:47:55.609
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: I’ve got a few words, and
Simon may have something but –

00:47:55.609 --> 00:47:56.609
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No, that’s fine.

00:47:56.609 --> 00:47:57.609
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Okay.

00:47:57.609 --> 00:47:59.349
I think I’d just pick up on that latter
point.

00:47:59.349 --> 00:48:05.799
I think the purpose of planning is not to
produce a plan that has the right number,

00:48:05.799 --> 00:48:10.180
whatever it is, at the right hand side; the
purpose of plans is to tell us what we are

00:48:10.180 --> 00:48:12.970
going to be able to do this year for our patients.

00:48:12.970 --> 00:48:20.940
And I think we are hopefully going to benefit,
we will benefit from doing this jointly and

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:27.150
seeing this as an NHS planning exercise rather
than the disjointed and disconnected commissioner

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:31.730
and provider – sometimes in direct conflict
– planning processes that we’ve had in

00:48:31.730 --> 00:48:32.730
the past.

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:35.550
But I think it’s important we do recognise
we’re on a journey here.

00:48:35.550 --> 00:48:45.560
So, thinking about it from a provider perspective,
it’s true that the plans in place for 17/18

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:51.859
did see a significant increase in electives
planned for Q4, yet at the same time, rightly,

00:48:51.859 --> 00:48:53.990
emergency patients had to be put first.

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:57.180
And those elective plans were therefore not
fulfilled.

00:48:57.180 --> 00:49:02.309
So we’re on a journey towards realistic,
honest planning, and my sense is that we’re

00:49:02.309 --> 00:49:03.369
moving in the right direction.

00:49:03.369 --> 00:49:07.920
We’re doing that jointly – we can only
do this jointly – but I think frankly, we’ve

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:11.559
still got some more work to do, even at this
late stage in the year.

00:49:11.559 --> 00:49:16.319
And I think I would just echo Richard’s
point: I mean, my strong belief is at the

00:49:16.319 --> 00:49:20.839
end of the day we have to have realistic plans
that are about realistic patient numbers that

00:49:20.839 --> 00:49:24.640
match capacity, staffing, with the money that
trusts have.

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:27.750
And that is an interesting conversation that
we then have about what we can do for our

00:49:27.750 --> 00:49:28.750
patients.

00:49:28.750 --> 00:49:32.730
And I think we’re getting there, but we’re
not quite there yet, and I think that needs

00:49:32.730 --> 00:49:35.220
to continue even though we’re in May.

00:49:35.220 --> 00:49:36.849
I think we need to get it right.

00:49:36.849 --> 00:49:37.890
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:42.880
And we need to face into the honest and difficult
conversations that those realistic plans present.

00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:47.089
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: I think it’s absolutely given:
we either have the difficult conversations

00:49:47.089 --> 00:49:51.499
at the beginning of the year, or we have them
later in the year when plans that previously

00:49:51.499 --> 00:49:53.390
have been set haven’t come to pass.

00:49:53.390 --> 00:49:57.039
So, that isn’t universally the case, but
where that is needing difficult, we have to

00:49:57.039 --> 00:49:58.039
have it now.

00:49:58.039 --> 00:49:59.470
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: But we have one conversation.

00:49:59.470 --> 00:50:00.470
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes.

00:50:00.470 --> 00:50:05.789
And I must say, it is hugely encouraging to
hear those that have many more years of planning

00:50:05.789 --> 00:50:11.990
experience in the NHS than I do saying how
much more joined up this is, that we are ahead.

00:50:11.990 --> 00:50:13.859
We need to maintain that momentum.

00:50:13.859 --> 00:50:19.529
So with that maintaining momentum, I think
we should get to the third item on the agenda,

00:50:19.529 --> 00:50:24.859
which in many ways sums up why we are all
sitting here together: a belief that it is

00:50:24.859 --> 00:50:31.880
in integrated care systems in local health
economies that the best possible outcomes

00:50:31.880 --> 00:50:35.670
for patients and citizens in the country reside.

00:50:35.670 --> 00:50:40.380
So Ben and Matthew, are you going to introduce
the paper briefly?

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:41.599
Emphasis on briefly.

00:50:41.599 --> 00:50:44.490
&gt;&gt;Ben Dyson: Yes, yes.

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:49.020
So, as the paper says, it’s about a year
now since both organisations – again, very

00:50:49.020 --> 00:50:54.680
much a joint endeavour between our two organisations
– began work with ten systems that essentially

00:50:54.680 --> 00:51:01.619
said: we want to come together across general
practice, hospital, community mental health

00:51:01.619 --> 00:51:05.180
services, across health and social care, across
physical and mental health.

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:09.749
We want to come together and take a really
quite fundamentally different approach to

00:51:09.749 --> 00:51:15.200
how we manage our collective resources, our
people, our estates, our community assets;

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:20.609
how we better use those collective resources
to improve quality of care for local communities,

00:51:20.609 --> 00:51:23.609
to improve health outcomes and to reduce inequalities.

00:51:23.609 --> 00:51:27.790
This has very much been a journey for them,
but during that time they’ve started to

00:51:27.790 --> 00:51:31.369
do some very exciting things, and this paper
can’t possibly start to do justice to the

00:51:31.369 --> 00:51:32.490
range of those things.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:38.849
They’ve started to do really exciting things,
to think about how you redesign care and support

00:51:38.849 --> 00:51:41.069
around the needs of individual patients.

00:51:41.069 --> 00:51:46.940
Typically, although these are generally quite
big integrated care systems, all the exciting

00:51:46.940 --> 00:51:51.710
work tends to happen at populations of a few
hundred thousand, or in a few cases, primary

00:51:51.710 --> 00:51:54.789
care networks covering 30, 40, 50,000.

00:51:54.789 --> 00:52:01.050
And their main focus has been on how they
use their resources better to provide better

00:52:01.050 --> 00:52:06.131
care and support for frail older people with
multiple long term health conditions; how

00:52:06.131 --> 00:52:11.461
you provide better holistic care and support
for people with mental health problems; how

00:52:11.461 --> 00:52:15.930
you make sense of what’s traditionally been
rather a higgledy-piggledy urgent care system.

00:52:15.930 --> 00:52:20.150
And even looking broader than that and thinking
very much about partnerships with local government

00:52:20.150 --> 00:52:24.779
and beyond, how you support local people into
employment.

00:52:24.779 --> 00:52:28.680
So, there’s a lot going on.

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:32.930
I think two particular reasons why it’s
important we brought this paper today.

00:52:32.930 --> 00:52:38.160
The first is that I think there’s a lot
that these integrated care systems can teach

00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:40.690
us about our joint working.

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:45.360
I think they will keep us honest in the sense
that they’re the ones who have said to us

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:50.400
throughout this journey, ‘This is how we’d
like the national leadership of the NHS to

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:51.400
act differently.’

00:52:51.400 --> 00:52:55.299
Not just in what we do and what we choose
to prioritise, but as was discussed earlier,

00:52:55.299 --> 00:52:56.910
in terms of the style of how we do it.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:53:03.519
I think we can use them as a sort of constant
weather vane for whether our joint working

00:53:03.519 --> 00:53:07.390
is having the impact and the value that we
want it to have for local health systems.

00:53:07.390 --> 00:53:10.990
But also, because they’ve gone through a
similar journey to the one we’re embarking

00:53:10.990 --> 00:53:14.740
on, having to think very much about their
cultures, their values and their mindsets,

00:53:14.740 --> 00:53:20.430
how they’ve come together to create common
purpose and then execute common, system wide

00:53:20.430 --> 00:53:21.430
plans.

00:53:21.430 --> 00:53:24.440
The second reason – this is the final thing
I’ll say – the second reason why we’ve

00:53:24.440 --> 00:53:30.270
brought this to the two boards today is to
announce the good news that there’ll be

00:53:30.270 --> 00:53:36.460
a further four local health systems joining
our integrated care programme.

00:53:36.460 --> 00:53:42.220
In one case, it’s worth just saying that
the population is very small: this is West,

00:53:42.220 --> 00:53:43.619
North and East Cumbria.

00:53:43.619 --> 00:53:48.440
But West, North and East Cumbria are very
much committed over the next year working

00:53:48.440 --> 00:53:53.910
with neighbouring sustainability and transformation
partnerships to scale up, so that by the following

00:53:53.910 --> 00:53:59.079
year – 2019/20 – the idea would be to
have an integrated care system covering the

00:53:59.079 --> 00:54:01.529
whole of Cumbria and the North East.

00:54:01.529 --> 00:54:07.599
The population size for Gloucestershire is
also slightly below the level we’d normally

00:54:07.599 --> 00:54:11.529
expect, but again there’s a commitment there
to work with neighbours to understand how

00:54:11.529 --> 00:54:15.230
they can collaborate better where it is necessary
to do so.

00:54:15.230 --> 00:54:19.580
We’ll also be welcoming West Yorkshire and
Harrogate, and Suffolk and North East Essex

00:54:19.580 --> 00:54:24.190
to the programme, and we’re very much looking
forward to working with them to carry on the

00:54:24.190 --> 00:54:27.779
journey that other integrated care systems
have been on over the last year.

00:54:27.779 --> 00:54:28.779
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Matthew?

00:54:28.779 --> 00:54:34.039
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I would just momentarily
emphasise the point that Ben touched on, about

00:54:34.039 --> 00:54:37.910
the extent to which this is a bottom-up rather
than a top-down set of changes.

00:54:37.910 --> 00:54:42.759
This began to an extent with the vanguard
programme and creating networks of GPs and

00:54:42.759 --> 00:54:45.720
focusing on 30–50,000 populations.

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:51.410
And what we learned out of that is that the
more you saw vertical integration, the more

00:54:51.410 --> 00:54:56.190
you saw horizontal integration, the more you
saw a population health approach to it, the

00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:57.579
more successful the vanguards were.

00:54:57.579 --> 00:55:04.239
And those have grown into our sustainability
and transformation programmes across the country,

00:55:04.239 --> 00:55:07.660
and the best of those are now becoming integrated
care systems.

00:55:07.660 --> 00:55:09.760
We’re hoping that they will all make it
to that.

00:55:09.760 --> 00:55:15.549
But as the NHS has worked closer and closer
together and putting traditional barriers

00:55:15.549 --> 00:55:18.749
aside to fix stuff, they’ve been looking
to us to do the same thing.

00:55:18.749 --> 00:55:23.400
And so there is a read-through, I think, directly
from the work that started with network to

00:55:23.400 --> 00:55:27.809
GPs and how do you focus on the needs of a
population, right through to the conversation

00:55:27.809 --> 00:55:30.869
we’re having today about why we’ve brought
two boards together.

00:55:30.869 --> 00:55:35.769
And I think if we remember that this is actually
about the delivery of services locally and

00:55:35.769 --> 00:55:40.809
not about organisational structures at each
of the levels, we will make more right decisions

00:55:40.809 --> 00:55:42.369
than we make wrong.

00:55:42.369 --> 00:55:44.749
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes, well said.

00:55:44.749 --> 00:55:48.200
Open it up; anyone would like to add to that?

00:55:48.200 --> 00:55:52.020
Simon, is there anything you’d like to…?

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:56.920
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: By going ahead with these
four, that’s 12 million people – sorry,

00:55:56.920 --> 00:56:00.410
more than 12 million people across England
now who will be beginning to benefit from

00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:03.420
the joined up services that Matthew’s just
described.

00:56:03.420 --> 00:56:11.549
So, we’re going to be publishing next month
the results of the final data from the vanguards.

00:56:11.549 --> 00:56:13.430
We talked about this a bit this morning.

00:56:13.430 --> 00:56:18.839
And I think what that will show decisively
is that not only does it improve patient experience

00:56:18.839 --> 00:56:23.670
and the quality of care, but it does help
relieve avoidable pressure on hospitals for

00:56:23.670 --> 00:56:26.079
emergency admissions.

00:56:26.079 --> 00:56:30.329
And the data now are quite compelling on that
point, and we will be publishing those, which

00:56:30.329 --> 00:56:34.539
I think will put more fuel in the tank of
the journey that we’re on.

00:56:34.539 --> 00:56:35.839
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Steve?

00:56:35.839 --> 00:56:40.070
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, so I’m going to repeat
the comments that I made yesterday in our

00:56:40.070 --> 00:56:46.069
private session, that I think what it’s
imperative that we do, nationally, regionally,

00:56:46.069 --> 00:56:51.130
with our nascent integrated care systems,
and with our patients and the public, is really

00:56:51.130 --> 00:56:56.760
double down on describing the narrative as
to what difference this will make if you’re

00:56:56.760 --> 00:57:00.160
a patient, if you’re a member of a local
community, if you’re a population.

00:57:00.160 --> 00:57:05.549
So we can set the expectations for integrated
care systems, so it’s clear what we want

00:57:05.549 --> 00:57:10.050
them to achieve, and actually probably more
importantly, we set with our patients and

00:57:10.050 --> 00:57:16.980
public an expectation as to what they want
to achieve and what they can expect out of

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:18.089
these changes.

00:57:18.089 --> 00:57:21.090
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: You made the point very powerfully,
yesterday, Steve.

00:57:21.090 --> 00:57:26.450
I think there is a real learning for us in
all of our joint working, that it’s important

00:57:26.450 --> 00:57:28.450
to describe what you mean.

00:57:28.450 --> 00:57:33.240
The danger is often we use the same words
and walk away thinking two completely different

00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:34.240
things.

00:57:34.240 --> 00:57:40.369
And the more we can describe in the terms
that our mums and dads and grannies will understand,

00:57:40.369 --> 00:57:45.250
because we’re speaking simple, comprehensible,
ordinary English and describing the change

00:57:45.250 --> 00:57:48.740
we want to see through the eyes of patients
and citizens in everything, whether it’s

00:57:48.740 --> 00:57:53.060
our joint venture or integrated care systems,
I think the better we will do.

00:57:53.060 --> 00:57:56.450
It’s a very powerful point for all of us.

00:57:56.450 --> 00:57:58.230
Any other final comments?

00:57:58.230 --> 00:58:02.230
Otherwise I think that’s rather a good place
to end the meeting as a whole.

00:58:02.230 --> 00:58:06.339
I do not know, Malcolm, if there’s anything
you’d like to add at the end?

00:58:06.339 --> 00:58:07.339
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: No I don’t have anything
to add.

00:58:07.339 --> 00:58:11.130
I think this is immensely encouraging, and
there’s huge wisdom around the table and

00:58:11.130 --> 00:58:13.960
a commitment to doing these things much better
than we’ve done in the past.

00:58:13.960 --> 00:58:19.529
But it’s not about these organisations;
it is about facilitating the growth of integrated

00:58:19.529 --> 00:58:23.930
care systems across the country, which come
from those who will lead them and will run

00:58:23.930 --> 00:58:24.930
them.

00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:31.279
So, the future operations for us and for those
who work for us is how we facilitate that,

00:58:31.279 --> 00:58:36.210
and how we refocus our efforts on trying to
bring that about, and make it easier for people

00:58:36.210 --> 00:58:40.539
who want to bring about beneficial change
locally to actually do so.

00:58:40.539 --> 00:58:44.109
So, thank you very much everybody for helping
us to get in that direction.

00:58:44.109 --> 00:58:49.050
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: So with that, I think we should
bring to a close our first joint NHS England

00:58:49.050 --> 00:58:50.740
and NHS improvement board meeting.

00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:55.019
I look forward to the second joint meeting
in September, but have no illusions: there

00:58:55.019 --> 00:58:59.259
is a lot of joint work for us to do, both
executives and non executives between now

00:58:59.259 --> 00:59:00.259
and then.

00:59:00.259 --> 00:59:04.359
And we will make sure that there are the right
gatherings and groupings to keep the momentum

00:59:04.359 --> 00:59:05.690
and, if anything, accelerate.

00:59:05.690 --> 00:59:06.619
So, thank you all very much.

